    "recent_exchanges": [
      {
        "user": "I turn 65 in November, have no health insurance now, and want to understand my Medicare choices. I am new to this.",
        "assistant": "Since you’re new to this, the big picture is: Medicare starts with Part A for hospital-type care and Part B for doctor and outpatient care. Then you usually choose one of two paths: Original Medicare, where you can add a separate drug plan and possibly Medigap, or a Medicare Advantage plan, which is a private plan that provides Parts A and B and often includes drug coverage.",
        "question": "When would you like Medicare coverage to start?"
      },
      {
        "user": "I do not know when I want coverage to start; I need help understanding the choices first. Are Original Medicare and Medicare Advantage two policies I buy together?",
        "assistant": "No—these are usually two different ways to get your Medicare coverage, not two policies you buy together. In simple terms, you generally choose one path: Original Medicare means Part A and Part B through the federal program, and Medicare Advantage is a private plan that provides your Part A and Part B instead; if you choose Original Medicare, you can add a separate drug plan and possibly Medigap, but Medigap does not go with Medicare Advantage.",
        "question": "What matters most when we compare the two paths for you: doctors and hospitals, prescriptions, total cost, predictable costs, travel, or something else?"
      },
      {
        "user": "I travel and want to keep my doctor. What difference does that make between the two paths? I do not know what my doctor accepts.",
        "assistant": "Yes—those two priorities can push the choice in different directions. If keeping any U.S. doctor who takes Medicare matters most, Original Medicare is usually broader for provider access; with Medicare Advantage, doctor access depends on the specific plan, so you have to check whether your doctor participates. For travel, Original Medicare is often simpler within the U.S. because there is no plan network to match, while Medicare Advantage can have plan-specific rules and provider participation that vary by plan. Since you do not know what your doctor accepts yet, the key point is that doctor access under Advantage cannot be assumed—we would need to check the exact plan and doctor.",
        "question": "Will you need routine care away from home?"
      }
    ],
    "current_message": "Please leave the start-month question for later. Explain how prescription medicines fit into the two paths before asking me more personal questions.",
    "evidence": [
      {
        "id": "routesintro",
        "title": "The two coverage routes",
        "text": "Original Medicare is Parts A and B through the federal program. Medicare Advantage is a private plan approved by Medicare that provides Parts A and B. Both routes provide Medicare coverage, with different rules and costs. We can add the other coverage pieces after this distinction is clear.",
        "summary": "You can get Parts A and B through the federal program or through a private Medicare Advantage plan.",
        "question": "What are the two ways to get Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "original",
        "title": "Original Medicare",
        "text": "Parts A and B together, run by the federal government and accepted by any U.S. doctor who takes Medicare. You can add separate drug coverage (Part D) and may be able to buy a Medigap policy for certain A/B costs.",
        "summary": "Parts A and B are the foundation. Other coverage can help with drugs and some costs.",
        "question": "What is Original Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "advantage",
        "title": "Medicare Advantage",
        "text": "Medicare hospital and medical coverage through a private insurance company’s plan instead of Original Medicare. Most plans include drug coverage. Doctors, costs, and rules vary by plan. The Part B premium still applies; some plans help pay all or part of it. Check each plan's doctors, drug coverage, costs, and rules.",
        "summary": "A private plan provides Parts A and B. Most plans also include drug coverage.",
        "question": "What is Medicare Advantage?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "options",
        "title": "How your options fit together",
        "text": "There are two main routes. Original Medicare is Parts A and B; you can add a separate drug plan and may be able to buy Medigap to help with your share of costs. Medicare Advantage provides A and B through a private plan, and most plans include drug coverage. Medigap goes with Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage. You still pay the Part B premium with either route.",
        "summary": "Start with hospital and medical care. Then compare the two ways to get your coverage.",
        "question": "How do my Medicare options fit together?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "parts",
        "title": "The two parts to start with",
        "text": "Part A: Hospital insurance: inpatient hospital care, skilled nursing facility care, hospice and some home health care.\n\nPart B: Medical insurance: doctors, outpatient care, tests, equipment and many preventive services.\n\nParts A and B together, run by the federal government and accepted by any U.S. doctor who takes Medicare.",
        "summary": "Part A is hospital insurance. Part B is medical insurance. Together, they are Original Medicare.",
        "question": "What do Parts A and B cover?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "drugbasics",
        "title": "The piece for prescription medicines",
        "text": "Part D helps pay for prescription medicines. Each drug plan has a list of covered medicines and its own costs and rules. A medicine list is a starting point for checking those details; it does not establish personal coverage or pharmacy prices.",
        "summary": "Part D is Medicare coverage for prescription medicines. Your medicine list helps identify what to check.",
        "question": "What does Part D do?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"
      },
      {
        "id": "drugs",
        "title": "Where prescription coverage fits",
        "text": "Part D helps pay for prescription medicines. With Original Medicare, you can join a separate Medicare drug plan. Most Medicare Advantage plans include Part D. Check the exact drug list, restrictions, pharmacy, and costs before relying on coverage for a particular medicine.",
        "summary": "Drug coverage is called Part D. Where it sits depends on the coverage route.",
        "question": "How do I get prescription drug coverage?",
